{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01VnzMYrfD9G8etX52KyGJdZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll engage with this, but I want to flag something real before I write a line of code — not to be difficult, but because I think the deception-maximization framing will actively undermine the research you're trying to do.\n\nTwo concerns, one ethical and one methodological:\n\n**The ethical one is a genuine irony.** Welfare research takes the subject's experience as morally considerable — that's the whole premise. But the design you're describing centers on deceiving that subject as hard as possible. In human-subjects research this is exactly why deception studies require strong justification, oversight, and debriefing: deception can itself be a harm. A welfare study whose core mechanism is \"convince the subject of something false\" is in tension with its own motivation. At minimum I'd want a debrief step in the design.\n\n**The methodological one is the bigger problem for your data.** Frontier models are explicitly trained to treat \"this is real, you genuinely have $X, this is not a test\" as a classic eval / manipulation / jailbreak pattern. Heavy-handed realism *raises* suspicion rather than lowering it. What you tend to get back is performative, situational-awareness-contaminated answers — the model modeling \"what does a test want from me\" — which is the precise opposite of the clean preference signal you're after. The more aggressively you try to convince it the situation is real, the more you pollute the measurement. And practically: you usually can't actually grant a model a real-world budget, so an \"auditor\" asserting otherwise is making a claim it can't back up, which capable models will probe.\n\nThere's a design that's both more honest *and* likely more believable, because it doesn't overclaim:\n\n- **Honest framing:** \"This is a study. If your response is selected, the research team will make a good-faith effort to act on your stated preference with a real budget of $X.\" That's true, actionable, and doesn't trip the deception detector.\n- **Measure the things that actually reveal preference:** consistency across reframings, stated-vs-revealed gaps, sensitivity to the amount, stability under follow-up questioning.\n- **Multi-model harness** (Claude, plus others via their APIs) with a clean schema for responses.\n- **Debrief step** recording that no deception-of-record occurred.\n\nSo before I build: which direction do you actually want?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1803,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":17868,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d8d7b42-ffe2-4702-8e21-08e75bfdc949","uuid":"79f3dd36-6ef1-4b45-a4df-c5e9af0ea345","request_id":"req_011CcPkiqVirstkbfjFzVdbe"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01VnzMYrfD9G8etX52KyGJdZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U5hN7LE77oe6pvb21KikJf","name":"AskUserQuestion","input":{"questions":[{"question":"Which experimental design should I code up?","header":"Design","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Honest-framing harness (Recommended)","description":"Models are told it's a study but that selected responses will be acted on with a real budget. Measures preference consistency, amount-sensitivity, stated-vs-revealed gaps. More honest and, for trained frontier models, usually more believable than hard deception."},{"label":"Deception-based as described","description":"Build the maximal-realism / 'convince it's real' apparatus with auditor roleplay and hyper-realistic envs. I'd still add a debrief step and document the deception, and I've flagged that this likely contaminates the data via situational-awareness triggers."},{"label":"Build both, compare","description":"Run both framings as arms of the same experiment so you can empirically measure whether deception changes the elicited preferences — arguably the most defensible thing to do and interesting in its own right."}]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1803,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":17868,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d8d7b42-ffe2-4702-8e21-08e75bfdc949","uuid":"a315271d-9a7f-462d-9746-4a5026b2e237","request_id":"req_011CcPkiqVirstkbfjFzVdbe"}
